    "falsification": "A null hypothesis of ICC <= 0.2 (or a similarly low threshold indicating poor reliability) would be tested. Falsification would involve demonstrating that the observed reliability coefficient is significantly and substantially greater than this threshold. Additionally, a placebo test could involve computing the 'reliability' of a randomly shuffled H2-H1 change metric or a metric known to be unreliable.",
    "multiplicity": "Only one primary estimand (the reliability coefficient for the chosen H2-H1 metric) would be tested. If multiple reliability metrics (e.g., split-half, test-retest, different performance metrics) are explored, pre-specify the primary one and adjust for multiple comparisons for secondary analyses.",
    "decision_rule": "Reject the null hypothesis (i.e., conclude the trait is reliable) if the lower bound of the 95% confidence interval for the ICC (or split-half coefficient) exceeds a pre-specified threshold of 'useful reliability' (e.g., 0.6-0.7) AND the p-value is < 0.05. Given historical evidence, this threshold is unlikely to be met.",
